S = {}
def s(k, t): S[k] = t

# ---- 12.6
s('6.s1', 'He says that in worshipping Me there is no such difficulty: "ye tu" etc.')
s('6.s2', 'The word "tu" signals a distinction from the preceding [case].')
s('6.s3', '"Ye tu" — those who, however —')
s('6.s4', '"All actions" (sarvāṇi karmāṇi).')
s('6.s5', '"In Me" (mayi).')
s('6.s6', '"Having renounced" (sannyasya) — having kept in mind that Hari alone, the independent Lord of all, abiding in me, has actions performed through me, the dependent agent, as His own worship; or [the sense is] "having offered" (samarpya).')
s('6.s7', '"Matparāḥ" — knowing "I alone am the highest."')
s('6.s8', '"With unswerving yoga alone" (ananyena eva yogena) — with meditation performed without worship of the ${T(\'avyakta\')}; that is, with meditation on Me alone.')
s('6.s9', '"Meditating on Me" (māṃ dhyāyantaḥ).')
s('6.s10', '"They worship" (upāsate).')
s('6.s11', 'Here, by saying "upāsate" [rather than "paryupāsate"], it is shown that this is not an exceptionally intense worship, like the worship of the unmanifest spoken of with "paryupāsate".')
s('6.s12', 'Intense restraint of the senses and the like is not required for My grace; hence there is no difficulty — this is hinted.')
s('6.s13', 'Likewise, by "na cirāt" ("before long"), it is hinted that there is no difficulty here either: whatever effort is needed for direct realization of the unmanifest — if they worship Me with that much, or even less, then direct realization of Me comes about.')
s('6.s14', 'By "teṣām ahaṃ samuddhartā bhavāmi" it is shown that even where sense-restraint and the like fall short, the Lord — just as the Devī [too] — does not neglect them; rather, He Himself gives those means, without effort [on their part], to the devotee. Thus the ease of the devotees\' worship of the Lord is exceedingly great — this is the sense.')
s('6.s15', 'Or [take it thus]: in the verse "kleśo \'dhikataraḥ…", by stating that the path of worship of the unmanifest is more difficult, it has been virtually stated, by implication of its purport, that My grace comes even when good conduct and the like fall short — since [My path] is free from such difficulty — and that the path of My worship alone is superior. So, by another reason also, He says that the path of worship of the Lord is superior: "ye tu" etc.')
s('6.s16', 'The first half has the same meaning as before.')
s('6.s17', '"Ananyena eva": the word "anya", here denoting other deities such as Lakṣmī, refers to [meditation] concerned with the independence and the retinue-status resting in them; that which is not that is "ananya" — "by that yoga", "by meditation".')
s('6.s18', 'That is: by meditation-yoga together with Lakṣmī, Brahmā and the others, as dependent on the Lord and as His retinue.')
s('6.s19', 'This follows from the sūtra "Oṃ aṅgāvabaddhās tu na śākhāsu hi prativedam Oṃ" and the like.')
s('6.s20', '"Meditating on Me, they worship" (māṃ dhyāyanta upāsate). ॥ 6 ॥')
# ---- 12.7
s('7.s1', '"Of them" — of those whose minds are absorbed in Me.')
s('7.s2', '"I."')
s('7.s3', '"From the ocean of death and saṃsāra" — from the ocean of saṃsāra, which has the nature of death.')
s('7.s4', '"Before long" (na cirāt) — quickly.')
s('7.s5', '"I become the deliverer" (samuddhartā bhavāmi) — this is the meaning.')
s('7.s6', 'Here, by saying "na cirāt", it is shown that when the Lord is worshipped together with Śrī, since the supreme grace of both is possible, liberation comes without delay.')
s('7.s7', 'But in the view of separate worship of the unmanifest, one first performs some worship of the Lord, then for a long time worships Śrī separately, and again must continually worship the Lord together with Śrī; only then does liberation come — thus it comes only after a long time. Therefore, because worship of the Lord leads to liberation in a short time, it follows that worship of the Lord alone is superior.')
s('7.s8', 'So it has been said: "Meditated upon together with Viṣṇu, she too attains supreme satisfaction" etc.')
# ---- 12.8
s('8.s1', 'Thus, in the manner stated, since worship of the Lord alone is supreme, do that alone.')
s('8.s2', 'If unable to do that, one should carry out other means for its attainment — so He says, in four [verses]: "mayi" etc.')
s('8.s3', '"Fix the mind on Me alone" — place [it] there.')
s('8.s4', 'That is, worship Me.')
s('8.s5', 'Failing that: "place the intellect in Me" —')
s('8.s6', 'acquire knowledge concerning Me.')
s('8.s7', '"Thereafter" — after the attainment of Me.')
s('8.s8', '"You will dwell in Me alone."')
s('8.s9', 'You will attain the state that consists in the experience of sorrowless bliss. ॥ 8 ॥')
# ---- 12.9
s('9.s1', '"Atha" — in the sense of another alternative.')
s('9.s2', '"Steadily" — in such a way that it is steady.')
s('9.s3', '"If you cannot fix the mind on Me."')
s('9.s4', '"Then" — because of the inability to fix the mind.')
s('9.s5', 'By the means characterized by ${T(\'abhyāsa-yoga\')} — the practice of fixing the mind on the Lord — through repeated ${T(\'pratyāhāra\')} (withdrawal [of the senses]).')
s('9.s6', '"To attain Me" — to know [Me]; "seek." ॥ 9 ॥')
# ---- 12.10
s('10.s1', '"If you are unable even for practice" (abhyāse \'py aśakto \'si).')
s('10.s2', 'Then "matkarmaparamaḥ": one for whom the performance of actions directed to Me, for My pleasure, is itself the highest and best — such a one.')
s('10.s3', '"Become."')
s('10.s4', 'To the question, "How can the desired accomplishment come by that much?" He says: "madartham" etc.')
s('10.s5', '"Even while doing" — this is the meaning. ॥ 10 ॥')
# ---- 12.11
s('11.s1', '"Atha" — as before.')
s('11.s2', '"Even this": if you are unable even to undertake [the thought that] all actions — worship, japa and so on — are directed to Me and are for My pleasure, then, performing actions with other deities as their aim and at the end offering them to Me — that is "My yoga", consisting of offering.')
s('11.s3', 'The means to having actions that are Mine — offering [them] to Me at the end.')
s('11.s4', 'Be one who has resorted to the means by which all actions are performed as belonging to Viṣṇu.')
s('11.s5', '"Then" — from that yoga of Mine.')
s('11.s6', '"Yatātmavān" — with a restrained mind.')
s('11.s7', '"Renounce the fruit of all actions" — this is the meaning.')
s('11.s8', 'The purport is that worship of Me alone must be done, in every way, by all who are represented by you [Arjuna]. ॥ 11 ॥')
# ---- 12.12
s('12.s1', 'He substantiates this: where the meditation spoken of in "mayi" etc. is not attained, there is knowledge; where that too is not attained, there is the yoga of practice — "śreyo hi" etc.')
s('12.s2', 'Knowledge is better than practice that is without knowledge.')
s('12.s3', 'Better than mere knowledge is meditation preceded by knowledge.')
s('12.s4', '"Than meditation" — that is, than meditation that is preceded by knowledge but is desire-laden (sakāma).')
s('12.s5', '"Renunciation of the fruit of action" is better.')
s('12.s6', 'It is to be construed thus: better than such desire-laden meditation is desireless meditation, marked by renunciation of the fruit of action.')
s('12.s7', '"Than renunciation" — that is, than the desireless meditation marked by renunciation of the fruit of action.')
s('12.s8', 'It is to be construed that direct (aparokṣa) knowledge is better [than that].')
s('12.s9', '"Immediately after, peace" — "peace" means liberation; this is the meaning. ॥ 12 ॥')
# ---- 12.13
s('13.s1', 'What was stated in "ye tu sarvāṇi karmāṇi" He elaborates by describing further qualities implied by it: "adveṣṭā" etc.')
s('13.s2', '"Hating no one" — not doing what is disagreeable [to anyone].')
s('13.s3', 'One who, having identified the source of another\'s distress, then protects [him] — "friendly" (maitra).')
s('13.s4', '"Compassionate" — merciful.')
s('13.s5', '"Free from the sense of \'mine\'" — devoid of the notion of independence in "this is mine."')
s('13.s6', '"Free from egoism" — devoid of the delusion of conceit about qualities that the self does not possess.')
s('13.s7', 'One for whom worldly pleasure, regarded as something to be shunned, is equal to pain — he is "samaduḥkhasukhaḥ."')
s('13.s8', '"Forbearing" — tolerant. ॥ 13 ॥')
# ---- 12.14
s('14.s1', '"Content" — one who has the sense of "enough" (alam).')
s('14.s2', '"Yogī" — one who has the means to knowledge in the form of action and meditation.')
s('14.s3', '"Self-controlled" — whose mind is withdrawn from the objects of the senses.')
s('14.s4', '"Firm in conviction" — one with a settled conviction of the truth, free from doubt as to its being unreliable (aprāmāṇya).')
s('14.s5', 'One whose mind and intellect are offered to Me — such a one is so called. ॥ 14 ॥')
# ---- 12.15
s('15.s1', 'Further: "by whom the world is not agitated" — the world does not fear him; he is not a cause of fear to the world.')
s('15.s2', 'And he who, having understood that all is under the Lord\'s control, is not agitated by the world, by people.')
s('15.s3', 'And [he who is free] from ${T(\'harṣa\')}, ${T(\'amarṣa\')}, fear and ${T(\'udvega\')}.')
s('15.s4', '"Harṣa" is the mind\'s inclination toward adharma out of intoxication (mada); "amarṣa" is anger.')
s('15.s5', '"Udvega" is a particular trembling of the mind.')
s('15.s6', '"Freed" by them — devoid of them.')
s('15.s7', 'Here, in "sa ca," the word "bhakta" is to be supplied.')
s('15.s8', '"Dear to Me." ॥ 15 ॥')
# ---- 12.16
s('16.s1', 'Further: "anapekṣaḥ"<FN1> — devoid of any expectation anywhere other than the Lord\'s pleasure.')
s('16.s2', '"Pure" — pure outwardly and inwardly.')
s('16.s3', '"Capable" — skilled in doing the Lord\'s work.')
s('16.s4', '"Impartial" — indifferent in the matter of benefit and injury [as things] to be done.')
s('16.s5', '"Free from distress" — not afflicted in mind by injuries done by others.')
s('16.s6', '"Renouncer of all undertakings" (sarvārambhaparityāgī) has four senses: a renouncer of all non-Vaiṣṇava undertakings; a renouncer of the conceit in all undertakings; a renouncer of the fruit of all undertakings; and one whose renunciation consists in offering all undertakings to the Lord. ॥ 16 ॥')
# ---- 12.17
s('17.s1', 'Since "harṣāmarṣabhayodvegair muktaḥ" ("free from elation, impatience, fear and agitation") conveys, by the suffix of the past participle (niṣṭhā), a sense of [their] having ceased, a doubt might arise that at a later time he may again be subject to elation and the like.')
s('17.s2', 'To dispel that, He makes it clear: "yo na hṛṣyati" etc.')
s('17.s3', 'By the sūtra "nānadyatanavat kriyāprabandhasāmīpyayoḥ"<FN3>, since the present-tense suffix is enjoined in the sense of continuity of the action, "hṛṣyati" etc. means "rejoices throughout life" and the like.')
s('17.s4', 'When the negation is joined, in the form "na hṛṣyati na dveṣṭi" etc., the meaning is "never rejoices" and so on — this is the idea.')
s('17.s5', 'And this has been cited in the Vṛtti, in the third pāda of the third [chapter].')
s('17.s6', '"Yāvajjīvam annam adāt" ("he gave food throughout his life") etc.')
s('17.s7', '"Sarvārambhaparityāgī" was said in general terms.')
s('17.s8', 'He makes it specific: "śubhāśubha" etc.')
s('17.s9', 'That is: a renouncer of auspicious things other than knowledge, devotion, dispassion and the like.')
s('17.s10', 'For this very reason, "does not desire" is to be understood as "does not desire anything other than that."')
s('17.s11', 'Likewise, it should be understood that "lokān nodvijate" is not redundant with "harṣāmarṣabhayair muktaḥ."')
s('17.s12', 'Because it serves to dispel the doubt stated. ॥ 17 ॥')
# ---- 12.18
s('18.s1', 'Further: "samaḥ śatrau ca mitre ca" etc.')
s('18.s2', 'Earlier, "samaduḥkhasukhaḥ" was said in general terms.')
s('18.s3', 'He makes that specific, by stating it in particular: "śītoṣṇa" etc.')
s('18.s4', 'In pleasures and pains born of objects such as cold and heat.')
s('18.s5', '"Equal" — devoid of exultation and dejection.')
s('18.s6', '"Free from attachment" — devoid of affection for the fruit. ॥ 18 ॥')
# ---- 12.19
s('19.s1', 'Further: "tulyanindāstutiḥ."')
s('19.s2', 'One for whom praise is equal to blame, in that [both] are to be shunned.')
s('19.s3', '"Aniketaḥ" — of no fixed residence.')
s('19.s4', 'Or: one who has the Lord, denoted by the letter "a," as his refuge.')
s('19.s5', '"Of steady mind" — whose knowledge is not shaken by fallacious reasoning (kutarka).')
s('19.s6', 'The repeated mention of devotion — "yo madbhaktaḥ," "bhaktimān" — is to show the superiority of devotion over the qualities stated in "adveṣṭā" etc.')
s('19.s7', 'Here, equality in pleasure and the like, and freedom from elation and the like, are to be understood as "for the most part" (prāyeṇa).')
s('19.s8', 'Because of [the statement] "For the most part equal in pleasure and the like, for the most part free from elation and the like" and so on.')
s('19.s9', '"Santuṣṭaḥ satatam" [14] and "santuṣṭo yena kenacit" [here] — the latter being of the nature of an explanation of the former, there is no redundancy. ॥ 19 ॥')
# ---- 12.20
s('20.s1', 'In "adveṣṭā sarvabhūtānām" etc., whatever was stated — non-hatred and so on — as a separate means merely to [My] pleasure, with "sa me priyaḥ," "sa ca me priyaḥ"<FN2> etc., all of that is [here] combined: "he in whom [all of it] exists is extremely dear to Me." Bundling together what was stated before, He concludes: "ye tu" etc.')
s('20.s2', '"Dharmyāmṛtam": dharma is Viṣṇu, because He upholds [everything].')
s('20.s3', 'Being an aid to His worship, [it is called] "dharmya" by figurative extension.')
s('20.s4', 'That which is a means to liberation, [here] called "amṛta" (immortality), is "amṛta."')
s('20.s5', 'That which is both dharmya and amṛta is "dharmyāmṛta."')
s('20.s6', '"As declared" — the whole [set of] non-hatred and the like stated by "adveṣṭā" etc., [which are] means of pleasing [Me].')
s('20.s7', '"Śraddadhānāḥ" — possessing faith (āstikya).')
s('20.s8', '"Matparamāḥ" — being knowers of My supremacy.')
s('20.s9', '"Those who faithfully follow" (ye paryupāsate) — practise it properly.')
s('20.s10', '"Those devotees."')
s('20.s11', '"Exceedingly dear to Me" — this is the meaning. ॥ 20 ॥')
s('20.s12', 'Thus the Twelfth Chapter, in the ${T(\'gītārthasaṅgraha\')} composed by Śrī Rāghavendra Yati.')

FN = {
6: [("२", "ब्र.सू. (३-३-५७)", "Brahma Sūtra (3.3.57) — the sūtra quoted above, \"aṅgāvabaddhās tu na śākhāsu hi prativedam.\"")],
12: [("२", "'ये भक्त्या विवशा विष्णोर्नाममात्रैकजल्पकाः । तेऽपि मुक्तिं व्रजन्त्याशु किमु तद्ध्यायिनः सदा ॥' इति व्योमसंहितायाम् । - भा.ता. (३/१०/१५)", "\"Even those who, overpowered by devotion, merely utter the Lord\\'s name alone, swiftly attain liberation — what then of those who always meditate on Him?\" — Vyoma-saṃhitā, as cited in Bhāgavata-tātparya (3/10/15).")],
13: [("२", "गायत्रीजपपरायणः - 'जप्येन एव तु संसिध्येत् ब्राह्मणः न अत्र संशयः । कुर्यादन्यन्न वा कुर्यात् मैत्रो ब्राह्मण उच्यते ॥' इत्युक्तेः ।", "[\"Maitra\" means] one devoted to Gāyatrī-japa — according to the statement: \"By japa alone a brāhmaṇa attains perfection; there is no doubt in this. Whether he does anything else or not, the one who is friendly (maitra) is called a brāhmaṇa.\"")],
16: [("१", "अनपः प्राणनाथः श्रीविष्णुः, तद्विषया ईक्षा ईक्षणं दर्शनं यस्य सः तथोक्तः । - इति पदरत्नावल्याम् १/२०/१ (श्रीविजयध्वजतीर्थश्रीचरणकृतायां भागवतव्याख्यायाम्)", "\"Anapa\" is Śrī Viṣṇu, the Lord of life; one whose vision (īkṣā, darśana) is directed to Him is so called [i.e., \"anapekṣa\"]. — Pada-ratnāvalī 1/20/1 (Śrī Vijayadhvajatīrtha's commentary on the Bhāgavata).")],
17: [("३", "पा. सू. ३-३-१३५", "Pāṇini Sūtra 3.3.135.")],
20: [("२", "चित्तद्रवस्तथा स्थैर्यं प्रसादो भक्तिलक्षणम् । आधिक्येन तु तत्रापि स्थैर्यमेव विशेषतः । दम्भस्य चलभक्तेश्च यस्मादश्रादिकं भवेत् । दम्भादिपरिहारार्थं निगृह्णीयाच्च धीरधीः । अत आध्यात्मिकक्लेशैराधिभूताधिदैविकैः । वाक्यैश्च वेदतन्त्राद्यैरुपदेशैश्च तादृशैः । बलवच्छासनैर्वाऽपि यस्य भक्तिर्न चाल्यते । स एव परमो भक्तो विष्णोर्हृदयवल्लभः ॥ - इति भक्तिविवेके ॥ - भा.ता. (११/२४/२४)", "\"Melting of the heart, steadiness, and serenity are the marks of devotion; and among these, steadiness is especially prominent. Because tears and the like may occur even in the hypocrite and the one of wavering devotion, the wise should restrain [them] to avoid hypocrisy and so on. Therefore, he whose devotion is not shaken by afflictions — ādhyātmika, ādhibhautika and ādhidaivika — nor by statements of the Veda, tantra and the like, nor by such teachings, nor even by forceful commands — he alone is the supreme devotee, the beloved of Viṣṇu's heart.\" — Bhakti-viveka, as cited in Bhāgavata-tātparya (11/24/24).")],
}
